From 31dd375b7bb6175bb704d5db6aa7ba817e767d44 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hottestad1 <hottestad1@bloomberg.net>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 14:40:39 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] GH-0000 Push a GROUP BY below a join with a
 one-row-per-key subquery in the LMDB native engine

A query that joins a subquery grouped by one variable (for example per-class
instance counts) with further patterns and then groups by that variable, the
subquery's outputs and more keys hid the aggregate's shape from the native
planner: the subquery cannot be flattened into the join, so specialised
strategies such as the type matrix were never offered and the whole join ran
through a single-threaded generic aggregate.

LmdbSubqueryGroupPushdownOptimizer rewrites
  Group(Join(Sub, Rest), K, A)
into
  Join(Sub, Projection[subquery](Extension[A](Group(Rest, K', A))))
when Sub has at most one row per join key c1, c1 is the only shared
variable and is always bound on both sides, every other Sub variable is a
grouping key, and no aggregate reads a Sub variable. The join then neither
duplicates nor reorders Rest rows, so every aggregate sees the same rows per
group. The aggregate extension elements the parser places above the group
move with it, so the inner subquery has the shape of a hand-written one.

The optimizer only runs in pipelines built by
LmdbNativeEvaluationStrategyFactory (new nativeEngine constructor flag), after
filters have been pushed onto their patterns.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
